From: Luma device integration <noreply@simplyluma.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: qrtr: smd: release send lock before failed endpoint cleanup

With the scoped opening/closing race fix, a failed rpmsg endpoint open
calls qrtr_endpoint_unregister() while still holding send_lock. The core
HELLO worker holds node->ep_lock while calling the transport xmit hook,
which waits for send_lock. Unregister waits for node->ep_lock in turn.

Keep registration and channel opening under send_lock, but release it
before unregistering a failed endpoint. The queued transmitter then sees
the NULL channel and returns -ENODEV, allowing teardown to complete.

Observed on Surface Pro 12-inch: IPCRTR udev workers and qrtr_hello_work
remain blocked after rpmsg reports that endpoint creation failed.
No Signed-off-by is supplied; this local device patch has not been
submitted upstream or physically qualified.

--- a/net/qrtr/smd.c
+++ b/net/qrtr/smd.c
@@ -78,14 +78,20 @@
 	if (rc)
 		return rc;
 
-	/* Block sending until we have fully opened the channel */
-	guard(mutex)(&qdev->send_lock);
+	/* Block sending until we have fully opened the channel. */
+	scoped_guard(mutex, &qdev->send_lock) {
+		rc = qrtr_endpoint_register(&qdev->ep, QRTR_EP_NID_AUTO);
+		if (rc)
+			return rc;
 
-	rc = qrtr_endpoint_register(&qdev->ep, QRTR_EP_NID_AUTO);
-	if (rc)
-		return rc;
+		qdev->channel = rpmsg_dev_open_ept(rpdev, qcom_smd_qrtr_callback, qdev);
+	}
 
-	qdev->channel = rpmsg_dev_open_ept(rpdev, qcom_smd_qrtr_callback, qdev);
+	/*
+	 * The HELLO worker can hold the endpoint lock while waiting for
+	 * send_lock. Drop send_lock before unregistering a failed channel,
+	 * because unregister also needs the endpoint lock.
+	 */
 	if (!qdev->channel) {
 		qrtr_endpoint_unregister(&qdev->ep);
 		return -EREMOTEIO;
